director paul thomas anderson's 1997  classic boogie nights takes the audience  on a vivid ride through the golden years  of the porn industry the story behind  the film however is almost as wild this  is the untold truth of boogie nights  paul thomas anderson's father ernie  guillardi anderson began his career in  the 1960s as a cleveland television  horror movie host however he eventually  found success as a hollywood voice  artist counting comedians actors and  other hollywood types among his friends  ernie had worked with tim conway at wjw  tv in cleveland in the 60s conway left  to star in mchale's navy but eventually  ernie joined him in los angeles and the  rest is history according to esquire  ernie eventually bought a big house in  the san fernando valley where he would  frequently play host to his various  funny friends like conway and actor  harvey corman both of whom had a flare  for twisted humor for paul's 12th  birthday ernie bought him a betamax  camcorder with the camcorder in hand  paul went on to make vignettes that  followed his father and his zany  hollywood friends according to esquire  hall's father likely inspired burt  reynolds jack horner and boogie night  there were other inspirations as well as  paul explained to the philadelphia city  paper growing up in the san fernando  valley porn was always around there were  these industrial looking buildings with  no signage but you'd see people going in  and out you knew what they were doing  anderson has been described by many of  the people involved in boogie nights as  looking like a kid when they first met  him which makes sense since he was 26 at  the time that he was looking to helm his  first big hollywood feature however  anderson had already made one movie  about dirk in the porn industry when he  was still in high school that film the  dirk diggler story was a visual first  draft for what he would finally realize  in boogie nights according to  grantland.com's oral history of the  movie paul thomas anderson claims to  have had an epiphany about the name dirk  digler at 17. he then wrote the name on  a piece of paper and began putting  together a student film using a  camcorder and a steadicam anderson  pulled together his friends to play dirk  reed and various others in the low  budget film he did have one star  involved though his dad ernie  back in the 90s john lesher was a  powerful talent agent one of his clients  was harmony kareem a talented young  writer whose career began when he wrote  kids at age 19. as deadline explained in  a 2013 article lesser championed kareem  for years while an agent at uta and  endeavour and leisure continued that  support as a producer yet lesher had  another wunderkind client at the time  paul thomas anderson michael deluca an  exec at new line cinema at the time told  grant land i was talking with john  lescher about the movie kids i passed  when he brought it to new line just as a  joke i said to him don't let me pass on  something this cool again and later john  said remember when you said don't let  you pass on something cool lesha then  described in the article how he met  deluca for drinks gave him the script  for boogie nights and the next day they  were in business according to  entertainment journalist sharon waxman  another reason for deluca to pick up  boogie nights may have been that he was  a quote bad boy at the time waxman told  grantland michael deluca loved that life  and he lived that life whether it was  good agenting or simply a case of party  boy meets party script it seems as  destined as dirk digler for greatness  at 23 anderson got into the sundance  film festival with his short film  cigarettes and coffee the following year  anderson came back to the sundance lab  and expanded on cigarettes and coffee  with his first feature heart eight paul  thomas anderson titled it sydney when he  first conceived the idea in high school  the idea for the film came after he  watched a short philip baker hall scene  as a consigliere named sydney in  midnight run sydney was anderson's  working title for the film and he was  infuriated when his backers took final  cut and the name away from him the movie  had a budget of 3 million dollars and  starred hall and john c riley as the  leagues gwyneth paltrow and samuel  jackson as top supporting actors plus  philip seymour hoffman early in his  career for a debut film from a 25 year  old kid heart 8 notched great reviews  and paved the way for anderson's first  studio feature the budget for boogie  nights was 15 million dollars and there  were more actors in sets but hall riley  and hoffman all returned to work with  anderson on a movie that would help  propel the careers of all four  despite the fact that the movie was an  at times depressing story about the porn  industry a lot of actors were circling  the script according to grant land's  oral history drew barrymore was roller  girl at one point the role of director  producer jack horner however was offered  to a lot of big talent bill murray  harvey keitel sydney pollock and albert  brooks were supposedly all offered the  role but paul thomas anderson discussed  it extensively with warren beatty i got  to know warren  many years ago about 20 years ago when i  was  going to do boogie nights i sent him the  script and asked him to be in it as  anderson said in an interview with  charlie rose warren had shown a lot of  interest in playing the part of jack  horner however anderson ultimately made  a realization that beatty didn't  necessarily want to play the director  anderson told grantland i started to  figure out that warren really wanted to  play dirk diggler mark wahlberg you  don't really want to play jack horner  you want to be the kid in this movie he  said yeah horner eventually went to burt  reynolds and it's hard now imagining  anybody else playing it though it  appears that reynolds brought quite a  few challenges to the production  in an interview with gq burt reynolds  was asked if he'd worked with anderson  again his answer i don't think so  personality wise we didn't fit according  to the grant land oral history there was  even a moment when anderson gave  reynolds perhaps less respect than he  deserved and it supposedly came to blows  burt reynolds clarified to conan o'brien  that he hadn't punched anderson but  wanted to i don't want to hit him in the  face  i just wanted to hit him  according to the hollywood reporter's  obituary for the actor reynolds was so  unhappy with the film and his place in  it that he fired his agent after seeing  it for the first time it was ironic then  that he won his only film golden globe  for his turn as jack horner plus he was  nominated for the best supporting actor  oscar although he eventually lost to  robin williams for his turn in goodwill  hunting  one of the most intense scenes in boogie  nights occurs towards the end when dirk  reed played by john c reilly and todd  parker played by thomas jane are sitting  in the living room of a crazy rich man's  house looking to rip him off by selling  him some fake dope in the scene there's  a random voyages walking around throwing  firecrackers at the floor many of the  casts recalled how that was the craziest  scene in the whole film and in large  part that was due to the unexpected  randomness of the constant mini  explosions director robert downey senior  said in grant land's oral history that  idea came from a scene in my movie  putney swope it's one of paul's  favorites my thing was the asian guy  who's throwing around firecrackers and  just doesn't say a word another  inspiration though was anderson's father  according to esquire ernie anderson  liked tossing firecrackers around so  there you have it two showbiz fathers  with a fondness for firecrackers  inspired one of the most insane scenes  in a movie full of intense sequences  a few characters or at least character  names made it to boogie knights from the  dirk dickler story one of those is reed  rothschild dirk's best friend in the  porn industry in the original though  reed was very different reed was played  by eddie dilcore a bodybuilder who was  also dirk's lover in that original  iteration del core features prominently  in the student film since he was dirk's  best friend there's even a scene where  they're both together in a hot tub while  the romantic element of the dirk reed  relationship didn't make it into boogie  nights one del core item did reid's hot  tub poem according to the oral history  del core was constantly trying to get  anderson to crack up on the set and was  even invited to be in boogie night  however by that point del cor had begun  his career as a christian minister which  would have been tough to square with a  film about drugs and porn one of his  improvisations was the poem that john c  reilly recites as reed while in the hot  tub with dirk it's a poem about love and  honey trees and bees one so bad yet so  amazing because of how well it sets up  the duo's rock solid friendship  i love you  you love me  going down the sugar tree did you write  that yeah i write songs too  anderson had been making movies since he  was 12 and some of those early ones were  actually scripted films in fact  anderson's early films all seemed to  march toward what he would eventually  realize in hard 8 and boogie nights  obviously the dirk digler story is a  major stepping stone from the tale of  dirk's rise and fall to the fact that  recording the touch was featured in both  as an example of dirk's failed second  career but according to esquire it  becomes striking how much of these films  began when anderson was still too young  to actually buy pornography esquire  mentions how anderson first told the  teacher he was going to make a movie  called sydney based solely on the  snippet of philip baker hall he'd seen  in midnight run as a young teen however  he made a miami vice parody called brock  landers which went on to be the name of  dirk's monumental x-rated action series  does this character have a name  his name  is brock landers  anderson also made a young guns parody  called young buns with erotic undertones  if nothing else anderson was a student  of film from a very early age and didn't  just want to watch them but wanted to be  part of them as well  john holmes was a very successful adult  film actor known for having a heavy drug  addiction and eventually his possible  role in the wonderland murders according  to esquire paul thomas anderson told a  friend in high school that the idea for  his next movie was inspired by the sad  life of holmes in a 1997 interview with  philadelphia city paper anderson  explained how dirk's career was very  similar to that of holmes with the 80s  and the drugs everything's taken a toll  on him that happened in john holmes's  life and it was reflected in the  character he played johnny watt we did  the same thing with dirk and in the  movie i blame the drugs and ego building  in that same interview he also  specifically mentions the early johnny  wad films as examples of great  storytelling in porn there was also a  john holmes documentary called exhausted  that was one of the inspirations for  anderson to shoot the dirk diggler story  as a mockumentary it was such a big  inspiration that the documentary was  even an added feature on a criterion  laserdisc of boogie night with anderson  pointing out in the commentary how the  film was a quote major influence  a final shocking fact to come out of  boogie nights is that its star a man  still known at the time as an underwear  model in teen idol called marky mark has  said that he probably wouldn't do boogie  nights again despite having just a few  acting credits at the time yes mark  wahlberg said in a 2017 appearance that  he hopes god forgives him for his poor  acting choices and that boogie knights  is at the top of the list there was a  lot of outcry about him negating the  movie that gave him serious acting cred  as julianne moore put it to andy cohen i  think he should thank god for boogie  nights  yes  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite stuff for coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]  you

